        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<video id="videoPlayer" controls></video>

<script>
    // Array of video parts to be played sequentially
    const videoSegments = ["part1.mp4", "part2.mp4", "part3.mp4", "part4.mp4"];
    let currentSegmentIndex = 0; // Start with the first segment

    // Get reference to the video element
    const videoPlayer = document.getElementById("videoPlayer");

    // Function to load and play the current segment
    function playSegment(index) {
        if (index < videoSegments.length) {
            videoPlayer.src = videoSegments[index];
            videoPlayer.play();
        }
    }

    // Event listener to load and play the next segment once the current one ends
    videoPlayer.onended = function() {
        currentSegmentIndex++;
        if (currentSegmentIndex < videoSegments.length) {
            playSegment(currentSegmentIndex); // Play the next segment
        } else {
            console.log("All video segments have finished playing.");
        }
    };

    // Initial play of the first segment
    playSegment(currentSegmentIndex);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:

1. **Array of Video Parts**: The `videoSegments` array holds the names of the video files in order. Replace `"part1.mp4"`, `"part2.mp4"`, etc., with the actual names of your video files.

2. **`playSegment` Function**: This function loads and plays a video segment based on the provided index.

3. **`onended` Event**: When a video segment finishes, the `onended` event is triggered. It increments `currentSegmentIndex` and calls `playSegment` to play the next video.

4. **Initial Play**: The first segment is loaded and played when the page loads.

### Tips:
- **File Path**: Ensure the video files are in the same directory as this HTML file or provide the correct relative paths.
- **Controls and Styling**: The `<video>` element is set to `controls` so users can play/pause if needed, and simple CSS styling centers the video on the page.

